<script lang="ts">
  export let onClose:()=>void;
  function open(node:HTMLDialogElement){node.showModal();return{destroy(){node.close()}}}
  const otherViewer = '\u0048akuraku';
</script>
<dialog use:open onclose={onClose} onclick={(event)=>{if(event.target===event.currentTarget)onClose();}} aria-label="FAQ">
  <header><h2>FAQ</h2><button aria-label="Close FAQ" onclick={onClose}>×</button></header>
  <div class="faq-body">
    <details><summary>What is Umalytics?</summary><p>It’s a replay and telemetry viewer for the Steam Global client. The Windows app can collect races while you play. This website viewer opens the same race JSON without installing anything. Either way you get the replay, stats, skills, hills, traffic, and the rest of the analysis.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Where do I download it, and where does it save files?</summary><p>The Windows app is <strong>Umalytics.exe</strong> from the GitHub releases page: <a href="https://github.com/exKitsune/Umalytics/releases/latest" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">github.com/exKitsune/Umalytics/releases/latest</a>. The web viewer is <a href="https://exkitsune.github.io/Umalytics/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">exkitsune.github.io/Umalytics</a>.</p><p>On Windows, races, settings, and the capture certificate key live in <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Umalytics</code>. Keep that folder private. Closing the window keeps collection running in the tray; use Exit in the tray menu to stop it.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How does race capture work?</summary><p>Umalytics only listens to game API traffic on your computer. It doesn’t scrape other apps or send your races anywhere. When the game returns a replay, we save that race JSON — the same kind of file you can import, export, or drop onto this window.</p><p>First-time setup is a one-time capture certificate plus a Steam launch option so the game talks through that local listener.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can I use the JSON with {otherViewer}?</summary><p>Yes. The exported race JSON is the same portable replay format {otherViewer} uses. You can take a race out of Umalytics and open it there, or bring a {otherViewer} JSON back here.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How accurate is the analysis?</summary><p>Umalytics uses an exact race simulator as its primary mechanics source when it reproduces every supported field in the recorded race. A matched run provides the simulator’s internal targets, status transitions, skill checks, random draws and spurt decisions, rather than requiring us to infer them from the velocity graph.</p><p>A match verifies the recorded fields at their captured precision; it cannot independently prove every hidden decision in the original game. Our derived analysis can still contain bugs or approximations. We aim for 99.9% accuracy, not a guarantee of perfection. If something looks wrong, please <a href="https://github.com/exKitsune/Umalytics/issues" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">open a GitHub issue</a> with the app version and what you observed.</p><p>If a simulation cannot run or does not match, the same dashboard uses the versioned inference engine. The top bar distinguishes Simulation matched from Inferred analysis.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What does Target speed show?</summary><p>For a matched race, the thin white/black line is the actual simulator target, including section randomness, skills, pace modes and slopes. Velocity approaches that target through acceleration, direct-speed effects and collision clamps. The movement explanation shows the executed speed change, target and acceleration at that tick.</p><p>On an inferred race, Simulated speed is the free-running reference path. Unknown inputs may leave ranges. Skill gains remove an effect from the local velocity calculation while keeping positions, HP and other decisions fixed; they do not rerun a different race.</p></details>
    <details open><summary>What does utilization mean?</summary><p>For target-speed skills: 0% while she’s blocked or still below normal speed, 50% while she’s accelerating from normal speed up to the boosted target, and 100% once she’s there. If she keeps some of that speed while slowing down, that still counts as full use. We compare paths with and without the skill, so leftover speed from something else doesn’t get counted twice.</p><p>Normal speed already includes her other skills and debuffs. This score is not the same as metres gained. Acceleration is useful while she’s still climbing toward the cap; sitting at the cap isn’t using it. Mixed skills show each effect separately.</p><p>The overall score averages the scored effectiveness of equipped skills; a skill that never activates counts as 0%. When speed skills overlap, earlier activations reach full credit first. Later skills receive half credit while accelerating toward their own ceiling.</p><p>For recoveries, HP efficiency is how much HP actually stuck after the cap. It doesn’t try to score the recovery’s whole-race value.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Why does recovery timing matter?</summary><p>A recovery can restore all its HP and still arrive after a delayed or slower spurt was selected. Matched races expose the actual spurt decisions and any recalculation; inferred races estimate those decisions from the replay.</p><p>The displayed HP budget and skill benefit are separate mathematical comparisons. Covering a theoretical budget is not the same as the engine selecting that target. Nearby speed changes can also come from skills, hills or traffic.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What is speed carryover?</summary><p>Speed effects can leave extra speed that still helps through a later acceleration or slowdown. That leftover distance is carryover. For acceleration skills, the full head start counts as direct gain even after the timer ends.</p><p>Carryover is counted once in the total. The percentage is leftover distance ÷ speed potential (full speed bonus × duration). A 0.5 m/s bonus for 4 seconds has 2 m of potential, so 0.4 m of carryover is 20%. Mixed skills only use their speed portion. Acceleration doesn’t get a carryover percentage.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How is skill duration calculated?</summary><p>Matched races use actual effect application and expiry ticks. Inference uses base duration × race distance ÷ 1000, with supported modifiers and recorded timing where available.</p><p>Green/passive skills appear as short, three-second activation markers on telemetry. Their effects still last for the race; the marker is not an expiry. Recoveries and other instant effects are also shown briefly for readability.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How does wit affect section speed?</summary><p>Each of the 24 distance sections has a wit-dependent random target-speed modifier. Matched races expose the actual executed modifier: see Stats → Actual section targets. These modifiers stop during the selected last spurt.</p><p>The expected section adjustment is a formula-derived average, shown separately from those actual draws. On an inferred race the draws remain unknown. Skills, acceleration and blocking affect whether the runner reaches the target.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Why is a result partial or unavailable?</summary><p>A matched simulator gives us its executed decisions and motion. Some dashboard quantities still ask a different question—how much distance a skill contributed, for example—and require a separate calculation. Unsupported effect types or incomplete calculation coverage remain unavailable.</p><p>Inference fallback has additional uncertainty because the replay does not record every hidden state. A range such as 83–100% use means the remaining time could not be classified; it does not mean the skill failed.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Are distance and time gains exact?</summary><p>They compare the replay to a calculation that removes one effect and leaves the other known effects alone. Time saved is the travel-time equivalent of the distance gain, not a predicted finish.</p><p>Don’t add overlapping skill and status gains together.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How are hills calculated?</summary><p>Uphill target-speed loss is slope × 200 ÷ power, with slope as a percentage. More power means less loss. The power comparison uses 1000 power.</p><p>Downhill mode adds 0.3 + |slope| ÷ 10 m/s and uses 40% of normal HP drain. Being on a downhill stretch doesn’t mean downhill mode is on.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How are outer-corner losses calculated?</summary><p>We compare her path to lane 0 around each corner. Extra distance comes from the recorded lane and the course’s turning angle. Equivalent time uses her recorded speed.</p><p>That’s 11.25 metres per lane unit, from game constants and the reference mechanics. It doesn’t include weaving or changed race decisions, so it isn’t an exact finish-time loss.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What does the Rushed bonus show?</summary><p>Rushed can bump the target by 4% of base strategy speed. The distance is how much of that extra speed she actually ran. Time spent blocked, or still below the ordinary target, adds nothing. While she’s still climbing onto the 4%, only the surplus she’s already reached counts. If we don’t know the spurt target, the window stays unavailable.</p><p>At the same speed, Rushed drains 1.6× normal HP per second (+60%). Spot Struggle + Rushed uses a combined 3.6× drain, or 7.7× for Runaway. These combined rates replace the individual rates; they are not multiplied together. Actual HP/s depends on speed, ground and phase.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Where do statuses come from?</summary><p>On matched races, downhill, pace, rushing, traffic, power release and other supported statuses come directly from simulator state or actual effect callbacks. They are not guesses from speed or HP. The status legend explains every displayed label and lets you select which bands appear below the graph. Last spurt and Inward start hidden in telemetry; replay still shows them. Choices are saved in this browser.</p><p>On inferred races, the replay directly reports some statuses, while downhill and pace modes are reconstructed from the available observations. Those estimates retain their uncertainty.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How are traffic and exhaustion shown?</summary><p>The Traffic band combines nearby and blocked intervals. Cuts below 0.15 m/s are hidden. The band smoothly fades from white at 0.15 m/s through yellow at 0.35 m/s and amber at 0.65 m/s to red at 1 m/s or more. It uses the larger of the self-speed and final-speed clamp cuts, never their sum. Transparent gaps are below the display threshold. Nearby-runner checks alone do not create a colored band. These cuts are separate from the difference between current and target speed; another effect can offset a cut. The threshold changes only the display, not the measured losses.</p><p>Distance losses compare local velocity paths with traffic removed or HP preserved, holding the surrounding race context fixed. They include speed recovery, can overlap, and are not exact finish-time penalties. Exhaustion losses begin when HP runs out and exclude earlier spurt delay.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What do HP spent, gained and stolen mean?</summary><p>Spent and gained are the downward and upward HP changes between samples. A recovery and drain in the same sample can partly cancel. Stolen combines incoming debuff damage with extra rushed drain; it is already part of spent, not an additional amount to add.</p><p>On inferred races, the first zero-HP time can be a range between samples. Matched races use the simulator’s HP timeline.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What are the start-quality labels?</summary><p>Gate delay is drawn between 0 and 100 ms. Great is under 20 ms; Good is 20–40 ms; Poor is over 40–80 ms; Late is over 80 ms.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How do you check debuffs?</summary><p>Matched races use actual effect delivery, immunity and application events to identify recipients. Movement and HP impact are calculated separately from those applied effects.</p><p>Inference fallback compares observed HP changes against normal drain and supported debuff effects. If more than one explanation fits, the hit remains uncertain.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Why didn’t a skill activate?</summary><p>Matched races show the actual wit check, conditions that were evaluated, prerequisite latches, cooldown readiness, activation and effect application. A short-circuited check was not evaluated; we do not label it failed just because another condition stopped the branch.</p><p>Inference fallback evaluates the conditions supported by the recording and separates unmet conditions from supported wit failures. Missing team, NPC or random-trigger data can leave the reason unknown.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What do the replay effects show?</summary><p>Skill bars show time remaining. Colored badges mark active statuses. Debuff badges use confirmed simulator deliveries on matched races, or supported replay recipients on inferred races.</p><p>Vision cones are the skill’s range. Someone inside a cone is a possible target; a recorded hit confirms it.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Which game version do the calculations use?</summary><p>Simulation and inference have separate version bindings. Known race dates can select a registered game era. Unknown dates may try the installed simulator, but its decisions are only shown after a complete supported-field match.</p><p>If matching fails, the full dashboard falls back to the authorized inference bundle. An unregistered historical race may use provisional current formulas; that does not establish historical mechanics compatibility.</p></details>
  </div>
</dialog>
